{"qid":"Q693570","names":["Duchy of Ferrara"],"title":"Duchy of Ferrara","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Duchy of Ferrara was ruled by the Este family, who held the city from 1240 and received formal ducal investiture from Pope Paul II in 1471 when Borso d'Este was granted the title. Under Ercole I (r. 1471–1505) Ferrara became a leading Renaissance centre—Ludovico Ariosto composed the Orlando Furioso at the Este court—and the city was expanded on a rational grid (the Addizione Erculea) that influenced later European urban design. The duchy ended abruptly in January 1598 when Pope Clement VIII invoked feudal reversion rights on the death of the heirless Alfonso II d'Este, annexing Ferrara directly to the Papal States; the Este cadet line retreated to Modena, which it retained as a separate duchy.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The duchy's political history and territorial extent are well-documented through Este court records and papal correspondence; boundaries fluctuated across the Po valley but are reasonably mapped for the major periods of Este rule.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Duchy of Ferrara","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duchy_of_Ferrara"},{"label":"Wikipedia: House of Este","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Este"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Ferrara","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferrara"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":null,"rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Obizzo III d'Este","nativeName":"Obizzo III d'Este","roman":"Obizzo III d'Este","start":1347,"end":1352,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Aldobrandino III d